    "https://careers.ucalgary.ca/jobs/18159078-assistant-or-associate-professor-teaching-and-research-faculty-of-nursing": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Teaching and Research), Faculty of Nursing",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Teaching and Research), Faculty of Nursing in \n            \n                Calgary,\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    AB,\n                \n            \n            Canada Skip to main content Menu Careers Home See All Jobs Candidate Information Total Rewards at UCalgary Workplace Diversity and Inclusion Indigenous Candidates Accessibility and Accommodation Application FAQ's Apply Now Require Authentication must be enabled to output referral button. Assistant or Associate Professor (Teaching and Research), Faculty of Nursing Job ID: 38409 Location: Main Campus Updated August 24, 2026 Apply Now Share: Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Share with Email Loading job Back to Search Results Previous Opportunity Next Opportunity We would like to take this opportunity to acknowledge the traditional territories of the people of the Treaty 7 region in Southern Alberta. The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). Description The Faculty of Nursing, University of Calgary, invites applications for 3 positions at the rank of Assistant Professor (tenure track) or Associate Professor (with tenure) . The appointments will ideally commence July 1, 2027, or at a mutually agreeable date. Candidates will be assessed against the qualifications and requirements for the advertised rank(s) for which they may qualify. Candidates must have a PhD in nursing or a related discipline and must be eligible for registration with the College of Registered Nurses of Alberta. Applicants at the Assistant rank will be early career academics who are establishing research programs, have previous professional and teaching experience, an emerging publication record, and promising capability in undergraduate and graduate-level teaching and graduate supervision. Applicants at the Associate rank will be mid-career nursing academics with a well-established track record of competitive research funding with demonstrated impact in their area of focus, demonstrated excellence in teaching within undergraduate and graduate programs, supervision of graduate students, evidence of established academic partnerships in professional and other communities, and engagement with colleagues in support and development of priority faculty initiatives. The Faculty of Nursing at the University of Calgary aims to be world leaders in women's health. Candidates at either rank must have a demonstrated focus on women's health within their particular area of specialization. Particular areas of interest for the faculty under the women's health umbrella include: Menstrual and sexual health Menopause and healthy ageing Women's heart health Mis/dis information Chronic pain Mental health and reproductive transitions Indigenous women's health Black and racialized women's health Innovation in women's health Position responsibilities The successful candidate(s) will establish or continue active programs of research, teach and mentor learners in undergraduate and graduate nursing programs, supervise graduate students, and contribute to academic service within the Faculty, University, profession, and broader community. Responsibilities will include developing and sustaining programs of research; disseminating scholarship through peer-reviewed publications and other scholarly outputs; classroom, clinical, and/or graduate-level teaching; student assessment, feedback, and mentorship; graduate supervision; curriculum and program contributions; and participation in Faculty and University service. The successful candidate(s) will foster respectful, inclusive learning and research environments and contribute to the Faculty's strategic focus on women's health through research, teaching, graduate supervision, and service. Further details about Teaching and Research Professoriate ranks can be found at: https://www.ucalgary.ca/hr/sites/default/files/teams/239/gfc-academic-staff-criteria-processes-handbook-2025.pdf (July 1, 2025 GFC Academic Staff Criteria and Processes Handbook) A complete application should be submitted in a single pdf, and include the following: Cover letter and curriculum vitae including the names and contact information for three referees; Statement outlining your program of research, particularly as it pertains to women's health; Statement of teaching philosophy/teaching dossier; Brief description of present and aspiring contributions to the field; and Reprints of two (2) representative academic publications. Information about the Faculty of Nursing can be found at our website: http://nursing.ucalgary.ca/ . Interested individuals should apply online via the 'Apply Now' link and applications should be addressed to: Dr. Catherine Laing Professor and Dean Faculty of Nursing University of Calgary Applicants are encouraged to apply by September 25, 2026, however positions will remain open until a suitable candidate(s) is found. The University of Calgary has launched an institution-wide Indigenous Strategy ii' taa'poh'to'p committing to creating a rich, vibrant, and culturally competent campus that welcomes and supports Indigenous Peoples, encourages Indigenous community partnerships, is inclusive of Indigenous perspectives in all that we do. As an equitable and inclusive employer, the University of Calgary recognizes that a diverse staff/faculty benefits and enriches the work, learning and research experiences of the entire campus and greater community. We are committed to removing barriers that have been historically encountered by some people in our society. We strive to recruit individuals who will further enhance our diversity and will support their academic and professional success while they are here. In particular, we encourage members of the designated groups (women, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities, members of visible/racialized minorities, and diverse sexual orientation and gender identities) to apply. To ensure a fair and equitable assessment, we offer accommodation at any stage during the recruitment process to applicants with disabilities. Questions regarding [diversity] EDI at UCalgary can be sent to the Office of Institutional Commitments ( oicteam@ucalgary.ca ) and requests for accommodations can be sent to People & Culture ( hrhire@ucalgary.ca ). All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. In this connection, at the time of your application, please answer the following question: Are you a Canadian citizen or a permanent resident of Canada? (Yes/No) The terms and conditions of this appointment, including salary, are governed by the collective agreement between the Faculty Association of the University of Calgary and the Governors of the University of Calgary. Detailed information is available here . For a listing of all academic opportunities at the University of Calgary, view our Academic Careers website . For more information about the Faculty of Nursing , click here . About the University of Calgary UCalgary is Canada's entrepreneurial university, located in Canada's most enterprising city. It is a top research university and one of the highest-ranked universities of its age. Founded in 1966, its 36,000 students experience an innovative learning environment, made rich by research, hands-on experiences and entrepreneurial thinking. It is Canada's leader in the creation of start-ups . Start something today at the University of Calgary. For more information, visit ucalgary.ca . The university's commitment to the Indigenous Strategy is evident through the oversight of 18 Elders serving on the Circle of Advisors and the many unit-based circles working towards implementation of the strategy including the Faculty Advisory Circle and teams such as the Office of Indigenous Engagement, the Writing Symbols Lodge, and the Indigenous Research Support Team. Many Indigenous-based events and processes are impacting curriculum, programming and polices at the University of Calgary. About Calgary, Alberta Calgary is one of the world's cleanest cities and has been named one of the world's most livable cities for years. Calgary is a city of leaders - in business, community, philanthropy and volunteerism. Calgarians benefit from a growing number of world-class dining and cultural events and enjoy more days of sunshine per year than any other major Canadian city. Calgary is less than an hour's drive from the majestic Rocky Mountains and boasts the most extensive urban pathway and bikeway network in North America. With a growing urban Indigenous population of approximately 50,000 people, there are several Indigenous organizations and many events and ceremonies taking place in the city throughout the year. Share: Share with Email Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Apply Now facebook twitter linkedin instagram youtube Digital Privacy Statement Privacy Policy Website Feedback University of Calgary 2500 University Drive NW Calgary Alberta T2N 1N4 CANADA Copyright © 2026 The University of Calgary, located in the heart of Southern Alberta, both acknowledges and pays tribute to the traditional territories of the peoples of Treaty 7, which include the Blackfoot Confederacy (comprised of the Siksika, the Piikani, and the Kainai First Nations), the Tsuut’ina First Nation, and the Stoney Nakoda (including Chiniki, Bearspaw, and Goodstoney First Nations). The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). The University of Calgary is situated on land Northwest of where the Bow River meets the Elbow River, a site traditionally known as Moh’kins’tsis to the Blackfoot, Wîchîspa to the Stoney Nakoda, and Guts’ists’i to the Tsuut’ina. On this land and in this place we strive to learn together, walk together, and grow together \"in a good way.\""
    },
    "https://careers.ucalgary.ca/jobs/18158580-sessional-instructor-master-of-physician-assistant-studies-cumming-school-of-medicine": {
      "title": "Sessional Instructor, Master of Physician Assistant Studies, Cumming School of Medicine",
      "description": "Sessional Instructor, Master of Physician Assistant Studies, Cumming School of Medicine in \n            \n                Calgary,\n            \n            \n                \n                \n                    AB,\n                \n            \n            Canada Skip to main content Menu Careers Home See All Jobs Candidate Information Total Rewards at UCalgary Workplace Diversity and Inclusion Indigenous Candidates Accessibility and Accommodation Application FAQ's Apply Now Require Authentication must be enabled to output referral button. Sessional Instructor, Master of Physician Assistant Studies, Cumming School of Medicine Job ID: 38407 Location: Foothills Campus Updated August 24, 2026 Apply Now Share: Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Share with Email Loading job Back to Search Results Previous Opportunity Next Opportunity We would like to take this opportunity to acknowledge the traditional territories of the people of the Treaty 7 region in Southern Alberta. The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). Description The Master of Physician Assistant Studies (MPAS) Program, Cumming School of Medicine , at the University of Calgary invites applications for a Sessional Instructor to serve as a Foundations Educator in the Master of Physician Assistant Studies (MPAS) Program. Term Dates: Friday, January 1, 2027 - Friday, December 31, 2027 Courses of Instruction: Winter 2027 (Monday, January 4, 2027 - Friday, April 30, 2027) MDPS 622 - Foundations of Practice II MDPS 630 - Foundations of Practice V Spring 2027 (Monday, May 3, 2027 - Friday, June 25, 2027) MDPS 623 - Foundations of Practice III - Part I MDPS 627 - Foundations of Practice VI - Part I Summer 2027 (Monday, June 28, 2027 - Friday, August 20, 2027) MDPS 624 - Foundations of Practice III - Part II MDPS 628 - Foundations of Practice VI - Part II Fall 2027 (Monday, August 30, 2027 - Friday, December 17, 2027) MDPS 621 - Foundations of Practice I MDPS 629 - Foundations of Practice IV The successful candidate will deliver high-quality instruction within the Master of Physician Assistant Studies (MPAS) curriculum through a combination of large-group teaching, small-group facilitation, experiential learning, simulation, and case-based instruction. Teach foundational and applied concepts that support the development of competent, compassionate, and practice-ready Physician Assistants. Develop instructional materials, learning activities, and educational resources that support student learning and engagement. Provide timely formative and summative feedback to learners and contribute to student assessment activities. Facilitate learning in areas including: Professional identity formation Foundations of physician assistant practice Professionalism Clinical reasoning Health systems and team-based care Other non-clinical competencies relevant to physician assistant education The successful candidate will serve as the primary educator responsible for delivery of major components of the longitudinal Foundations of Practice curriculum. In collaboration with the Medical Director and other faculty, the successful candidate will coordinate the delivery of the curriculum across both years of the program, ensuring continuity of learning and integration of professional competencies with students' evolving clinical knowledge and experiences. Responsibilities include: Coordinate the delivery of the Year One and Year Two Foundations of Practice curriculum in collaboration with the Medical Director and other faculty. Develop and deliver instructional sessions that integrate professional identity formation, physician assistant practice, professionalism, health systems, ethics, and other foundational non-clinical competencies. Develop and facilitate sessions that prepare students for transition into clinical practice and clerkship. Facilitate Year Two clinical review sessions that support integration of students' clerkship experiences through case-based discussions, diagnostic reasoning, evidence-informed practice, and reflective learning. Collaborate with clinical faculty to ensure alignment between didactic and clinical learning experiences. Support curriculum mapping, course evaluation, and continuous quality improvement activities consistent with accreditation standards. Lead the integration of narrative medicine, reflective practice, and humanistic approaches throughout the curriculum. Develop educational activities that promote empathy, patient-centred care, resilience, communication, and reflective practice. Facilitate reflective discussions and longitudinal professional development exercises that foster professional identity formation and lifelong learning. Serve as a mentor for physician assistant students throughout the program. Provide academic guidance, coaching, and professional mentorship. Support students in achieving academic and professional success through individualized guidance and timely feedback. Participate in student individualized learning plans, as appropriate. Develop, administer, and evaluate learner assessments aligned with course learning objectives. Participate in assessment review, standard setting, and continuous improvement of evaluation processes. Contribute to Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs), simulation-based assessments, and other competency-based evaluation activities. Contribute to the ongoing development, implementation, and evaluation of the MPAS curriculum. Participate in curriculum review, educational innovation, and quality improvement initiatives. Contribute to accreditation activities, curriculum mapping, and program evaluation. Engage in scholarly activities related to physician assistant education, health professions education, or educational innovation, as appropriate. Participate in faculty meetings, curriculum committees, admissions activities, and other educational initiatives as assigned. Collaborate with faculty, clinical preceptors, and community partners to support the successful delivery of the MPAS curriculum. Contribute to a collegial and collaborative academic environment that supports excellence in physician assistant education. Required Qualifications Graduate of an accredited Physician Assistant program in Canada or the United States. Eligible for registration as a Physician Assistant in Alberta. Greater than 10 years of experience teaching Physician Assistant students within an accredited Masters-level Physician Assistant program. Greater than 15 years of experience supervising Physician Assistant students in the clinical setting. Experience developing curriculum and/or teaching evidence-based medicine, narrative medicine, and interprofessional education. Excellent communication, interpersonal, and collaborative skills. Commitment to learner-centred education and continuous quality improvement. Preferred Qualifications Experience in curriculum development, instructional design, or learner assessment. Experience in health professions education or faculty development. Experience in narrative medicine, reflective practice, humanities in medicine, or professional identity formation. Demonstrated interest in educational scholarship, curriculum innovation, or program evaluation. Application Instructions Interested individuals are encouraged to apply online via the \"Apply Now\" link. Please be aware that the application process allows a maximum of four (4) attachments, which may require you to merge documents. Your application should contain the following: Cover letter and curriculum vitae Concise statement of teaching philosophy Teaching evaluations or evidence of teaching effectiveness Contact information for three (3) referees or three (3) letters of reference Questions may be addressed to: Chenwei Lian, MPAS Program Manager, mpas@ucalgary.ca Applications are accepted until September 10, 2026 . The University of Calgary has launched an institution-wide Indigenous Strategy ii' taa'poh'to'p committing to creating a rich, vibrant, and culturally competent campus that welcomes and supports Indigenous Peoples, encourages Indigenous community partnerships, is inclusive of Indigenous perspectives in all that we do. As an equitable and inclusive employer, the University of Calgary recognizes that a diverse staff/faculty benefits and enriches the work, learning and research experiences of the entire campus and greater community. We are committed to removing barriers that have been historically encountered by some people in our society. We strive to recruit individuals who will further enhance our diversity and will support their academic and professional success while they are here. In particular, we encourage members of the designated groups (women, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities, members of visible/racialized minorities, and diverse sexual orientation and gender identities) to apply. To ensure a fair and equitable assessment, we offer accommodation at any stage during the recruitment process to applicants with disabilities. Questions regarding [diversity] EDI at UCalgary can be sent to the Office of Institutional Commitments ( oicteam@ucalgary.ca ) and requests for accommodations can be sent to People & Culture ( hrhire@ucalgary.ca ). All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. In this connection, at the time of your application, please answer the following question: Are you a Canadian citizen or a permanent resident of Canada? (Yes/No) The terms and conditions of this appointment, including salary, are governed by the collective agreement between the Faculty Association of the University of Calgary and the Governors of the University of Calgary. Detailed information is available here . For a listing of all academic opportunities at the University of Calgary, view our Academic Careers website . For more information about the Cumming School of Medicine , click here . About the University of Calgary UCalgary is Canada's entrepreneurial university, located in Canada's most enterprising city. It is a top research university and one of the highest-ranked universities of its age. Founded in 1966, its 36,000 students experience an innovative learning environment, made rich by research, hands-on experiences and entrepreneurial thinking. It is Canada's leader in the creation of start-ups . Start something today at the University of Calgary. For more information, visit ucalgary.ca . The university's commitment to the Indigenous Strategy is evident through the oversight of 18 Elders serving on the Circle of Advisors and the many unit-based circles working towards implementation of the strategy including the Faculty Advisory Circle and teams such as the Office of Indigenous Engagement, the Writing Symbols Lodge, and the Indigenous Research Support Team. Many Indigenous-based events and processes are impacting curriculum, programming and polices at the University of Calgary. About Calgary, Alberta Calgary is one of the world's cleanest cities and has been named one of the world's most livable cities for years. Calgary is a city of leaders - in business, community, philanthropy and volunteerism. Calgarians benefit from a growing number of world-class dining and cultural events and enjoy more days of sunshine per year than any other major Canadian city. Calgary is less than an hour's drive from the majestic Rocky Mountains and boasts the most extensive urban pathway and bikeway network in North America. With a growing urban Indigenous population of approximately 50,000 people, there are several Indigenous organizations and many events and ceremonies taking place in the city throughout the year. Share: Share with Email Share on Twitter share to twitter Share on Facebook share to facebook Share on LinkedIn share to linkedin Apply Now facebook twitter linkedin instagram youtube Digital Privacy Statement Privacy Policy Website Feedback University of Calgary 2500 University Drive NW Calgary Alberta T2N 1N4 CANADA Copyright © 2026 The University of Calgary, located in the heart of Southern Alberta, both acknowledges and pays tribute to the traditional territories of the peoples of Treaty 7, which include the Blackfoot Confederacy (comprised of the Siksika, the Piikani, and the Kainai First Nations), the Tsuut’ina First Nation, and the Stoney Nakoda (including Chiniki, Bearspaw, and Goodstoney First Nations). The City of Calgary is also home to Métis Nation within Alberta (Nose Hill Métis District 5 and Elbow Métis District 6). The University of Calgary is situated on land Northwest of where the Bow River meets the Elbow River, a site traditionally known as Moh’kins’tsis to the Blackfoot, Wîchîspa to the Stoney Nakoda, and Guts’ists’i to the Tsuut’ina. On this land and in this place we strive to learn together, walk together, and grow together \"in a good way.\""
